page("what-is-a-collapsible-charcoal-grill","What Is a Collapsible Charcoal Grill?","A plain-English explanation of collapsible (flat-pack) charcoal grills: how the plates lock together, why they pack flat, and what they do well.","c28",f"""
<p>A collapsible charcoal grill is a grill that comes apart. Instead of a welded box or a bowl on legs, it is made of flat panels that join together into a firebox when you want to cook and separate again when you are done. Packed, it is a stack of plates. Set up, it is a solid grill that holds charcoal or wood, takes a grate or griddle on top, and stands up off the ground.</p>
<h2>The basic parts</h2>{ul(["<b>End pieces</b> that form the short walls and often the legs.","<b>Side panels</b> that form the long walls and the floor of the firebox.","<b>A bottom or spine</b> on some designs that stiffens the body and catches ash.","<b>Grates</b> that sit on or inside the top of the body, and on some grills <b>griddle plates</b> that swap in for a grate."])}
<p>On the <a href="{COMPACT}">Slot Grill</a>, the panels join with tabs that drop into slots (that is where the name comes from). There are no hinges, no bolts to thread before cooking and no tools. The Slot Grill's makers say most owners set it up or take it down in about 30 to 60 seconds once they have done it a couple of times.</p>
<h2>Collapsible, folding, flat-pack: same thing?</h2><p>People use the words loosely. In practice there are two families:</p>{table(["Type","How it packs","What to know"],[["Hinged folding grills","Legs or walls fold on hinges or pins","Fast to open; hinges and pins are the parts that loosen or bind over time"],["Flat-pack (knock-down) grills","Separate plates come fully apart and stack","Nothing to wear out at a joint; you learn a short assembly order"]])}<p>The Slot Grill is the second kind. For more on the trade-offs see {L("flat-pack-vs-folding-vs-fixed-grills","flat-pack vs folding vs fixed grills")}.</p>
<h2>What a collapsible grill does well</h2>{ul(["<b>Storage.</b> A flat stack slides behind a seat, under a bed, into a camp box or onto a garage shelf. The Compact packs to about 1&quot; thick and the Full Size to about 1.25&quot;.","<b>Real fire.</b> It is a charcoal grill, not a stove. You get direct heat, smoke and sear.","<b>Choice of fuel.</b> Both Slot Grill models burn charcoal or clean cooking wood.","<b>Cleaning.</b> Flat plates are easy to scrape, wipe and oil."])}
<h2>What it asks of you</h2><p>Raw carbon steel needs a little care: season it, keep the cooking surfaces lightly oiled and store it dry. The grill has no lid, so you cook with direct and indirect zones rather than closing the cover; see {L("two-zone-fire-collapsible-grill","two-zone fires")}. And like every charcoal grill, it goes on a stable, non-combustible surface outdoors.</p>
""","basics",[("Is a collapsible grill as sturdy as a fixed one?","It depends on the steel. The Slot Grill uses 12-gauge carbon steel on the Compact and 10-gauge on the Full Size, and its makers state both are strong enough for cast iron."),("Does a collapsible grill need tools?","Not the Slot Grill. It assembles by hand with tab and slot, with no tools and no hinges.")])

page("how-tab-and-slot-assembly-works","How Tab-and-Slot Assembly Works","Why tab-and-slot steel holds together under heat without bolts or hinges, and what to check each time you assemble a flat-pack grill.","c13",f"""
<p>Tab-and-slot is an old metalworking idea: one part has a tongue (the tab), the other has a matching opening (the slot), and when the tab passes through the slot the two parts are locked in position. With thick, precisely cut steel, a handful of tabs can hold a whole box rigid. The <a href="{SPECS}">Slot Grill specs page</a> describes it simply: the steel panels assemble by hand and hold each other in place.</p>
<h2>Why the V shape matters</h2><p>On the Slot Grill the two side panels interlock into a tight V between the end pieces. A V is a strong shape: the weight of charcoal and food pushes the sides into the joint rather than prying them apart. The remaining side panel's top tabs sit on the end pieces and its three bottom tabs slide into the first side panel, so the whole body is triangulated.</p>
<h2>What heat does to the joints</h2><p>Steel expands when hot. A grill with tight bolted joints can bind or warp at the fasteners; a slotted joint has a little designed clearance, so the parts can move slightly as they heat and cool without fighting each other. The thickness of the steel is what keeps it from flexing. That is one reason the Slot Grill uses 12- and 10-gauge carbon steel rather than thin stamped sheet.</p>
<h2>The check that matters</h2>{ol(["Every tab fully through its slot, not riding on the edge.","End pieces with the SLOT GRILL text facing out.","The grill sitting level, not rocking on a corner.","Nothing on the grill bearing a load before the last tab is seated."])}
<p>If something feels stiff, back it out and line it up again rather than forcing it. A little ash or seasoning in a slot can make a tab tight; a quick wipe fixes it. Full assembly steps are in {L("first-setup-step-by-step","first setup, step by step")}.</p>
""","basics",[("Can tab-and-slot joints come loose while cooking?","When every tab is fully seated and the grill is level, the parts hold each other in place. Don't lift or move a lit grill; let it cool first.")])

page("flat-pack-vs-folding-vs-fixed-grills","Flat-Pack vs Folding vs Fixed Charcoal Grills","How flat-pack, hinged folding and fixed portable charcoal grills compare for storage, setup, durability and cooking, without the hype.","both02",f"""
<p>If you want a charcoal grill you can move around, you are choosing between three broad designs. None is best for everyone. Here is how they compare on the things that matter after the first month.</p>
{table(["","Flat-pack (knock-down)","Hinged folding","Fixed portable"],[["Packed shape","Flat stack of plates","Folded unit, usually thicker than flat-pack","Same shape packed as in use"],["Setup","Short assembly order","Unfold and lock","None"],["Wear points","Few; no hinges","Hinges, pins and latches","Legs and handles"],["Cleaning","Plates come apart","Corners and hinges trap ash","Bowl or box"],["Storage space","Least","Moderate","Most"]])}
<h2>When flat-pack wins</h2><p>When space is the constraint: a car trunk, a kayak hatch, a camp box, a shelf in an apartment storage closet. When you want something that gets used hard and has no small parts to fail. When you like being able to take a grill fully apart to clean it.</p>
<h2>When it doesn't</h2><p>If you want to set the grill down and light it with zero assembly every single time, a fixed grill is simpler. If you want a lid for long low-and-slow cooks, a lidded cooker is the tool. A lidless flat-pack grill excels at direct grilling, griddle cooking and fire-and-ice style cooking, and can do longer cooks with good fire management, but it isn't an oven.</p>
<h2>Material matters more than style</h2><p>A thin flat-pack grill can be worse than a thick fixed one. Look at steel thickness first; see {L("steel-gauge-and-why-it-matters","steel gauge and why it matters")}. The Slot Grill uses <a href="{COMPACT}">12-gauge carbon steel on the Compact</a> and 10-gauge on the Full Size.</p>
""","basics",[("Which portable grill type packs smallest?","A flat-pack grill usually packs smallest because the parts come completely apart and stack. The Slot Grill Compact packs to about 1 inch thick.")])

page("carbon-steel-grills-explained","Carbon Steel Grills Explained","What carbon steel is, why it is used for collapsible grills, how it differs from stainless, and why it needs seasoning.","c08",f"""
<p>Carbon steel is iron with a small amount of carbon and very little else. It is the same family of metal as a carbon steel wok or a blacksmith's skillet: strong, easy to cut precisely, tolerant of high heat, and able to take on seasoning. Both Slot Grill models are carbon steel; the makers state plainly there is no stainless steel version.</p>
<h2>Why use it for a grill</h2>{ul(["<b>Strength at thickness.</b> Thick carbon steel stays rigid when it is hot and loaded with coals and cast iron.","<b>Heat.</b> It shrugs off the heat of a charcoal or wood fire.","<b>Seasoning.</b> Oil heated onto carbon steel polymerizes into a dark, slick coating that protects it and helps food release.","<b>Repairable finish.</b> If it rusts on the surface, you scrub and re-season. There is no coating to chip off for good."])}
<h2>Carbon steel vs stainless</h2>{table(["","Carbon steel","Stainless steel"],[["Rust","Rusts if left wet and bare; seasoning protects it","Resists rust"],["Care","Keep oiled and dry","Wipe and go"],["Seasoning","Takes seasoning well","Doesn't hold seasoning the same way"],["Look over time","Darkens to a patina","Discolors with heat"]])}
<p>The trade is simple: carbon steel asks for a minute of oil after a cook, and in return you get thick, strong steel that you can restore with a scrub pad. See {L("seasoning-a-new-collapsible-grill","seasoning a new grill")} and {L("surface-rust-on-carbon-steel","dealing with surface rust")}.</p>
<h2>Body vs cooking surfaces</h2><p>Slot Grill's own FAQ says the makers like high-temperature BBQ paint on the grill body and keep the grilling surfaces lightly oiled. Their <a href="{BALM}">Seasoning Balm</a> page also describes the balm as a food-safe alternative to paint for the body. Never put paint on grates or griddle plates.</p>
""","basics",[("Is the Slot Grill stainless steel?","No. Both models are carbon steel, and there is no stainless version."),("Will a carbon steel grill rust?","Bare, wet carbon steel will rust. Light surface rust is cosmetic: clean it off and re-season.")])

page("steel-gauge-and-why-it-matters","Steel Gauge and Why It Matters on a Portable Grill","What 10-gauge and 12-gauge mean, why thicker steel holds up better in a collapsible grill, and how gauge affects weight.","full08",f"""
<p>Sheet steel thickness is often given as a gauge number. The counterintuitive part: <b>a lower gauge number means thicker steel.</b> 10-gauge is thicker than 12-gauge, which is thicker than 16-gauge, and so on. Slot Grill's Full Size page puts 10-gauge in everyday terms: about the thickness of two stacked quarters.</p>
<h2>Why thickness matters in a collapsible grill</h2>{ul(["<b>Rigidity.</b> A flat-pack grill's strength comes from the plates themselves. Thick plates don't flex when you load coals and a heavy pan.","<b>Warping.</b> Thin steel heated unevenly tends to warp. Thick steel is far more stable.","<b>Heat holding.</b> Thick steel stores heat, which helps a grate stay hot when cold food hits it.","<b>Life span.</b> Thin steel can burn through in a hot firebox over years of use; thick steel has more to give."])}
<h2>The weight trade-off</h2><p>Thicker steel is heavier. The two Slot Grills show it clearly:</p>{table(["Model","Steel","Weight (grill only)"],[[f'<a href="{COMPACT}">Compact</a>',"12-gauge carbon steel","About 14 lb"],[f'<a href="{FULL}">Full Size</a>',"10-gauge carbon steel","About 27.6 lb"]])}
<p>The Compact is the same design at 80% scale in a slightly thinner gauge, to make it lighter to carry while staying strong enough for cast iron. The Full Size uses the heavier steel for larger cooks and heavier loads. See {L("compact-vs-full-size-slot-grill","Compact vs Full Size")}.</p>
""","basics",[("Is 10-gauge thicker than 12-gauge?","Yes. Lower gauge numbers mean thicker steel.")])

page("air-gap-and-airflow-in-a-collapsible-grill","Air Gap and Airflow in a Collapsible Grill","How the space under a flat-pack grill's coal bed affects airflow, heat on the surface below, and how to set up safely on a table or tailgate.","full10",f"""
<p>Fire needs air from below. A grill that sits flat on the ground with coals right on the floor tends to smother and to cook the surface it sits on. Collapsible grills solve this by raising the coal bed. Slot Grill calls the distance from the table to the bottom of the coal bed the <b>air gap</b>.</p>
{table(["Model","Air gap","Height"],[["Slot Grill Compact","2⅞&quot;","10.5&quot;"],["Slot Grill Full Size","3⅝&quot;","13&quot;"]])}
<p>The makers say the air gap helps minimize radiant heat to the surface below, and the elevated firebox improves airflow. "Minimize" is the key word: it is still a live charcoal fire, and the surface under it still has to be non-combustible.</p>
<h2>Using airflow to control heat</h2><p>A lidless grill has no vents to adjust, so you control heat with fuel and placement:</p>{ul(["<b>More coals, closer together</b> = hotter.","<b>Spread coals out</b> = cooler and longer-lasting.","<b>Leave an empty zone</b> for indirect cooking; see {0}.".format(L("two-zone-fire-collapsible-grill","two-zone fire")),"<b>Empty old ash</b> before each cook; ash blocks air.","<b>Wind</b> acts like a bellows. Turn the grill or shield it; see {0}.".format(L("wind-and-weather-collapsible-grill","wind and weather"))])}
<h2>Safe surfaces</h2><p>Good: bare soil, gravel, sand, concrete, pavers, a fire-safe pad. Not good: wood decks, plastic tables, vinyl, dry grass, truck bed liners or boat decks. If you must cook on a table, make it a metal or stone table with a heat-resistant pad, and keep the grill well clear of the edge.</p>
""","basics",[("Can I put a collapsible grill on a wooden table?","Not directly. Use a non-combustible surface. The air gap reduces radiant heat, but it does not make a combustible surface safe.")])
